{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat packet-claims-2.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"n\": 31,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The column reports that NVIDIA's latest reported quarter revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin 74.9% and its operating margin roughly 66%, set against Micron's fiscal Q3 2026 figures of 346% revenue growth, 84.6% GAAP gross margin and 80.4% operating margin; it adds that NVIDIA still earns more dollars in absolute terms. The item does not state which NVIDIA quarter these figures cover.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031; the spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, i.e. a broader aggregate than the big four hyperscalers' own data center capex.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% revenue growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7% and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%. Micron is a high-bandwidth memory supplier into the AI accelerator supply chain whose pricing and output bear on NVIDIA's memory input costs and supply.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs — a named buyer confirming memory and storage cost inflation in the hardware supply chain that also feeds AI accelerator and server bills of materials.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 32,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's next-generation Rubin Ultra rack-scale systems—the Kyber NVL144 architecture and the larger NVL576 system—begin shipping or reach volume availability by the end of 2028?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments or volume availability of Nvidia's Rubin Ultra rack-scale systems (Kyber NVL144 and NVL576), confirmed by Nvidia disclosure or an official shipment/product-availability announcement.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028-12-31 check date, based on the item's report that Kyber has been delayed to 2028; NVL576 is also reported as likely delayed or volume-limited.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028. SemiAnalysis attributed the delay to manufacturability challenges with the multi-layer PCB midplane in the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture was delayed and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together for similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, and said this left Nvidia with no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 33,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and Nvidia claims the group will spend more than $1 trillion the following year, per the Fool.com author.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 34,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"In a 7 July 2026 fool.com article, author Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year and that next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he said would drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The 7 July 2026 fool.com article said Nvidia has a strong read on data center build-out plans because many clients are placing orders for its devices well in advance of when they need them, so they can bring them online quickly once data center infrastructure is ready.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia has a strong read on data center build-out plans, as many clients are placing orders for devices well in advance of when they need them so they can quickly get them online once the data center infrastructure is ready.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The 7 July 2026 fool.com article said supply of NAND and DRAM memory has not kept up with data center-driven demand, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed (benefiting Micron), and that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later — leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"There has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 35,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-mem-prices-2026\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will memory prices — the high-bandwidth memory (HBM) and DRAM that AI accelerators and consumer devices depend on — rise over calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The change in HBM/DRAM contract prices paid by AI chipmakers and device makers during calendar 2026, as shown by memory contract-price releases and memory makers' reported pricing and margins.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026; the item gives no release date, so the period is the calendar year its memory-shortage and memory-cost discussion covers, checked against memory contract-price releases for Q4 2026 published in early 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's latest reported figures, cited by Allen Harris in a comparison with Micron, showed revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%; the item does not say which quarter these NVIDIA figures cover, and notes that NVIDIA \\\"still earns more dollars\\\" than Micron.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs, cited by Allen Harris, estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% revenue growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a GAAP gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and an operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%), as cited by Allen Harris; the item frames Micron as a memory maker supplying the memory that sits alongside AI accelerators.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-mem-prices-2026\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, according to Allen Harris.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-mem-prices-2026\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 36,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. It puts memory content at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. These are Morgan Stanley estimates, not NVIDIA disclosures.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis, reported by MarketScale, says first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter. This is a third-party schedule estimate, not an NVIDIA-confirmed shipment.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis, reported by MarketScale, attributes the Vera Rubin rack's cost to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, notes contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and estimates that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack. These are Morgan Stanley estimates and market commentary.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 37,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-memory-prices-2027\",\n    \"question\": \"Will DRAM and HBM memory contract prices be higher in calendar 2027 than in 2026, given that new memory capacity is not expected to come online until 2027 or later and Micron expects tight market conditions to last until 2028?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The direction and level of DRAM/HBM contract prices through calendar 2027 (quarterly contract-price changes), the key input-cost backdrop for NVIDIA's GPUs and the industry's memory supply.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item: new capacity \\\"won't be online until 2027 or later\\\" and inventory stays low for \\\"at least the next year and a half\\\" from the article date of 7 July 2026, with Micron saying tight conditions could last until 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-capex-650b-1t\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-micron-tight-2028\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in its most recent quarterly announcement as reported by fool.com, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside what the article calls huge growth and strong guidance for the following quarter (attribution is to Micron's announcement, relayed by the article).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-capacity-2027\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article's author (fool.com, Keithen Drury) forecasts that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half, and that this could push memory chip prices even higher because demand is not slowing.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices-2027\",\n     \"clean-8\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 38,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In its bill-of-materials analysis, Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory supply against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 39,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that in calendar 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), according to the article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 40,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by the article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-micron-q3fy26\",\n    \"summary\": \"Memory maker Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 41,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimated that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimated Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley put memory content at about $2 million per Vera Rubin rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the rack bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to Morgan Stanley's analysis reported by MarketScale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, and that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 42,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. These are analyst bill-of-materials estimates, not disclosed NVIDIA prices or a reported transaction.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, reported by MarketScale, puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) rack first shipments are reported as scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per MarketScale's article drawing on Morgan Stanley's analysis. This is a shipment schedule/plan, not a confirmed shipment.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 43,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Columnist Allen Harris (Berkshire Edge) reports NVIDIA's own latest-quarter financial metrics alongside Micron's: NVIDIA revenue growth of 85% (versus Micron's ~346%), GAAP gross margin of 74.9% (versus Micron's 84.6%) and operating margin of roughly 66% (versus Micron's 80.4%), adding that NVIDIA still earns more dollars. The period of NVIDIA's figures is not specified beyond 'latest'.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge, who attributes the AI-driven memory demand to NVIDIA's success spilling over into Micron.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 44,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"In a fool.com column, analyst Keithen Drury stated that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year), and projected that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-shortage\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported in its recent results that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028; the article adds that memory chip prices have surged because supply has not kept pace with the data-center build-out and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\",\n     \"There has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-8\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-micron-revenue-guidance\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided its next quarter to $50 billion in revenue, which the article states is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-8\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 45,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; as a share of bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is what is driving up memory and rack costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the article's account of Morgan Stanley Research's analysis, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 46,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported third-quarter fiscal 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that Nvidia's latest revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin was 74.9%, and its operating margin was roughly 66%, compared with Micron's 346% growth, 84.6% gross margin, and 80.4% operating margin; it adds that Nvidia still earns more dollars.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 47,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet that houses its Rubin Ultra chips and carries the high end of its data-center value — reach first customer shipments by the end of 2028?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments/deployments of NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or a cloud customer's deployment announcement.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028 check date, based on the item's report (published 2026-07-05) that Kyber has been delayed to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with the next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at its core remains challenging to manufacture.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack architecture was delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said a backup plan to bolt two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" which it said could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 48,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to debut with its 2027 Vera Rubin Ultra chips (and the NVL576 eight-rack optical system) — reach first shipments in calendar 2027, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber (and the related NVL576 scale-up system) ships in 2027 with Vera Rubin Ultra, versus the reported >12-month delay to 2028.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — the year Kyber/NVL576 were slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra; the outcome (2027 first shipments vs. a 2028 slip) can be checked through NVIDIA's launch/shipment disclosures during 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028. SemiAnalysis attributed the setback to manufacturing difficulty with the PCB midplane, a specialized multi-layer printed circuit board connecting electronic modules, saying 'Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.'\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576, a larger NVIDIA system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been cancelled after pushback from cloud service providers and hyperscalers over the design and operational burden. The firm said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.'\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report on the Kyber delay and said, 'Our roadmap is intact,' maintaining that its product plan and schedule still stand.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 49,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\",\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned for this year (2026), according to the Motley Fool article, which attributes the next-year figure to a claim by Nvidia.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 50,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\",\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, and that Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (2027). Both figures are spending projections/claims, not confirmed outlays.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 51,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers (Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta) plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, as reported by fool.com's analyst article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), a step up from the roughly $650 billion they plan to spend in 2026, according to fool.com's article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 52,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, according to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent before; memory rises from 5–10% of the GB200 bill of materials to 25–30% on the VR200 while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%, and Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In the Morgan Stanley Vera Rubin bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, first Nvidia VR200 NVL72 shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is a force now raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and tight memory availability now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation for AI rack builds.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 53,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 54,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether proposed U.S. legislation/regulation to phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States is enacted (and its scope and timing).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The fool.com article's author states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, a figure that could exceed $1 trillion in 2027, and that this will drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology announced that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, as reported by the fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The fool.com article states that memory chip prices have skyrocketed on strong data center-driven demand this year, and that new memory capacity at many industry players will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping inventory in low supply for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 55,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture (paired with its Vera Rubin Ultra data-center system) begin shipping in 2027 as originally planned, or is it delayed to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Vera Rubin Ultra rack system in 2027 versus a slip to 2028.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 check date, based on the item's statement that Kyber had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, with SemiAnalysis reporting a delay to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a Monday post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane at the system's heart remains challenging to manufacture.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis also said NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan, bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power, has been cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back over its odd design and heavy operational burden.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber architecture had been delayed, saying, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 56,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra data-center chips, begin shipping in calendar 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether first Kyber (Rubin Ultra) rack shipments occur in calendar 2027 or are delayed to 2028.\",\n    \"period\": \"Whether Kyber ships in calendar 2027 (original plan) or slips to 2028 — check at end of calendar 2027, the year the item says it had been slated to debut.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-kyber-delay\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-backup-plan-cancelled\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together for similar compute was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving Nvidia with what the firm called \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\"; it predicted this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-nvidia-denial\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report of a Kyber delay, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-rubin-shipping\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-dc-revenue-estimate\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 57,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144), the cabinet that debuts with Vera Rubin Ultra and carries the 2027 high-end platform's value, reach first shipments — the step that brings revenue — by the end of calendar 2027, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra systems reach first shipments by the end of calendar 2027.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of calendar 2027: the 2027 debut the item says Kyber had been slated for, and which SemiAnalysis now reports has slipped more than 12 months to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips with 144 GPUs per unit, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, attributing the slip to manufacturability problems with the multi-layer PCB midplane at the system's core; it added that the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together to reach comparable scale-up power for Rubin Ultra has been cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, which the firm said leaves Nvidia with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra and could open a rare high-end technical opening for AMD and Google's in-house chips.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report on Kyber delays and said its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 58,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates, in a bill-of-materials analysis, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the generation-over-generation increase.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content in Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory rose from 5 to 10 percent of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis has first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale writes that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it says memory availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\",\n     \"With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 59,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-memory-price\",\n    \"question\": \"What will average DRAM (memory chip) contract prices be in calendar 2027, given industry supply that the item says stays tight for at least the next year and a half and possibly until 2028?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The average DRAM/memory contract price level in calendar 2027 versus 2026, a key input cost for NVIDIA's GPUs (HBM/DRAM), reflecting whether tight memory supply keeps lifting NVIDIA's memory input costs.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2027 (the item's 'next year'); the shortage is described as lasting 'at least the next year and a half' with tight conditions possibly until 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-hyperscaler-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article (Keithen Drury, fool.com) states that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026), and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which the author says will drive even higher demand for NVIDIA Gpus.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-shortage\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that memory demand from the data-center build-out has outstripped supply and driven memory chip prices up sharply; it states that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving supply tight for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher, and relays Micron's statement that tight market conditions could last until 2028. DRAM is described as high-speed memory used by NVIDIA's GPUs, so this is an input-cost signal for NVIDIA.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"There has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\",\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-advance-orders\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that many of NVIDIA's clients are placing orders for devices well in advance of when they need them, so they can bring capacity online quickly once data-center infrastructure is ready, which the author says gives NVIDIA a strong read on data-center build-out plans.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia has a strong read on data center build-out plans, as many clients are placing orders for devices well in advance of when they need them so they can quickly get them online once the data center infrastructure is ready.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 60,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber-2028\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack that houses Rubin Ultra GPUs and carries the next generation of its high-end AI systems — reach first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in calendar 2028, as SemiAnalysis now reports, rather than in 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber NVL144 reaches first shipments with Vera Rubin Ultra in calendar 2028, i.e. whether the more-than-12-month delay to 2028 reported by SemiAnalysis holds or Nvidia's 'roadmap is intact' denial is borne out.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2028 — the year the item reports Kyber is now delayed to, versus a planned 2027 debut with Vera Rubin Ultra; set by the item's reported delay date.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a Monday post that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture — the 144-GPU cabinet designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028; it had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. The cause, per SemiAnalysis, is manufacturability of the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-2028\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-2028\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar scale-up power — was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving Nvidia with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.' The firm said that could give AMD and Google, whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n     \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-2028\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the report, Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a larger Nvidia system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-2028\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-2.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([{k: v for k,v in e.items() if k \"'!= \"kept_evidence\"} | {\"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\":x[\"id\"],\"summary\":x[\"summary\"],\"links\":x[\"links\"]} for x in e[\"kept_evidence\"]]} for e in p], ensure_ascii=False))'\"' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-2.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([{k: v for k,v in e.items() if k \"'!= \"kept_evidence\"} | {\"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\":x[\"id\"],\"summary\":x[\"summary\"],\"links\":x[\"links\"]} for x in e[\"kept_evidence\"]]} for e in p], ensure_ascii=False))'\"' \"","aggregated_output":"[{\"n\": 31, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The column reports that NVIDIA's latest reported quarter revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin 74.9% and its operating margin roughly 66%, set against Micron's fiscal Q3 2026 figures of 346% revenue growth, 84.6% GAAP gross margin and 80.4% operating margin; it adds that NVIDIA still earns more dollars in absolute terms. The item does not state which NVIDIA quarter these figures cover.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031; the spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, i.e. a broader aggregate than the big four hyperscalers' own data center capex.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% revenue growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7% and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%. Micron is a high-bandwidth memory supplier into the AI accelerator supply chain whose pricing and output bear on NVIDIA's memory input costs and supply.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs — a named buyer confirming memory and storage cost inflation in the hardware supply chain that also feeds AI accelerator and server bills of materials.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 32, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\", \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\", \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"new-kyber-nvl144\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"], \"stored_links\": [\"new-kyber-nvl144\", \"clean-2\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-1\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's next-generation Rubin Ultra rack-scale systems—the Kyber NVL144 architecture and the larger NVL576 system—begin shipping or reach volume availability by the end of 2028?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments or volume availability of Nvidia's Rubin Ultra rack-scale systems (Kyber NVL144 and NVL576), confirmed by Nvidia disclosure or an official shipment/product-availability announcement.\", \"period\": \"2028-12-31 check date, based on the item's report that Kyber has been delayed to 2028; NVL576 is also reported as likely delayed or volume-limited.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028. SemiAnalysis attributed the delay to manufacturability challenges with the multi-layer PCB midplane in the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture was delayed and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-1\", \"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together for similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, and said this left Nvidia with no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 33, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\", \"source_passages\": [\"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\", \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-9\", \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\", \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and Nvidia claims the group will spend more than $1 trillion the following year, per the Fool.com author.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 34, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\", \"source_passages\": [\"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"In a 7 July 2026 fool.com article, author Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year and that next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he said would drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"The 7 July 2026 fool.com article said Nvidia has a strong read on data center build-out plans because many clients are placing orders for its devices well in advance of when they need them, so they can bring them online quickly once data center infrastructure is ready.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"The 7 July 2026 fool.com article said supply of NAND and DRAM memory has not kept up with data center-driven demand, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed (benefiting Micron), and that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later — leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 35, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\", \"source_passages\": [\"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-mem-prices-2026\", \"question\": \"How much will memory prices — the high-bandwidth memory (HBM) and DRAM that AI accelerators and consumer devices depend on — rise over calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"The change in HBM/DRAM contract prices paid by AI chipmakers and device makers during calendar 2026, as shown by memory contract-price releases and memory makers' reported pricing and margins.\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2026; the item gives no release date, so the period is the calendar year its memory-shortage and memory-cost discussion covers, checked against memory contract-price releases for Q4 2026 published in early 2027.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's latest reported figures, cited by Allen Harris in a comparison with Micron, showed revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%; the item does not say which quarter these NVIDIA figures cover, and notes that NVIDIA \\\"still earns more dollars\\\" than Micron.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs, cited by Allen Harris, estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% revenue growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a GAAP gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and an operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%), as cited by Allen Harris; the item frames Micron as a memory maker supplying the memory that sits alongside AI accelerators.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem-prices-2026\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, according to Allen Harris.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem-prices-2026\"]}]}, {\"n\": 36, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"E2\", \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"E5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"E4\", \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\", \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-2\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. It puts memory content at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. These are Morgan Stanley estimates, not NVIDIA disclosures.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis, reported by MarketScale, says first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter. This is a third-party schedule estimate, not an NVIDIA-confirmed shipment.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis, reported by MarketScale, attributes the Vera Rubin rack's cost to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, notes contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and estimates that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack. These are Morgan Stanley estimates and market commentary.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 37, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\", \"source_passages\": [\"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-memory-prices-2027\", \"question\": \"Will DRAM and HBM memory contract prices be higher in calendar 2027 than in 2026, given that new memory capacity is not expected to come online until 2027 or later and Micron expects tight market conditions to last until 2028?\", \"outcome\": \"The direction and level of DRAM/HBM contract prices through calendar 2027 (quarterly contract-price changes), the key input-cost backdrop for NVIDIA's GPUs and the industry's memory supply.\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item: new capacity \\\"won't be online until 2027 or later\\\" and inventory stays low for \\\"at least the next year and a half\\\" from the article date of 7 July 2026, with Micron saying tight conditions could last until 2028.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-capex-650b-1t\", \"summary\": \"fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-micron-tight-2028\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in its most recent quarterly announcement as reported by fool.com, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside what the article calls huge growth and strong guidance for the following quarter (attribution is to Micron's announcement, relayed by the article).\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-prices-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-capacity-2027\", \"summary\": \"The article's author (fool.com, Keithen Drury) forecasts that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half, and that this could push memory chip prices even higher because demand is not slowing.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-prices-2027\", \"clean-8\"]}]}, {\"n\": 38, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\", \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\", \"clean-3\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"In its bill-of-materials analysis, Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"The item reports that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory supply against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 39, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\", \"source_passages\": [\"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\", \"source_passages\": [\"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that in calendar 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), according to the article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 40, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by the article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-micron-q3fy26\", \"summary\": \"Memory maker Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%).\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}, {\"n\": 41, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\", \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\", \"clean-3\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimated that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimated Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley put memory content at about $2 million per Vera Rubin rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the rack bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to Morgan Stanley's analysis reported by MarketScale.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, and that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 42, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\", \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"memory-prices\", \"clean-3\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. These are analyst bill-of-materials estimates, not disclosed NVIDIA prices or a reported transaction.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, reported by MarketScale, puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) rack first shipments are reported as scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per MarketScale's article drawing on Morgan Stanley's analysis. This is a shipment schedule/plan, not a confirmed shipment.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}, {\"n\": 43, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\", \"source_passages\": [\"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Columnist Allen Harris (Berkshire Edge) reports NVIDIA's own latest-quarter financial metrics alongside Micron's: NVIDIA revenue growth of 85% (versus Micron's ~346%), GAAP gross margin of 74.9% (versus Micron's 84.6%) and operating margin of roughly 66% (versus Micron's 80.4%), adding that NVIDIA still earns more dollars. The period of NVIDIA's figures is not specified beyond 'latest'.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge, who attributes the AI-driven memory demand to NVIDIA's success spilling over into Micron.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 44, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\", \"source_passages\": [\"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-capex\", \"summary\": \"In a fool.com column, analyst Keithen Drury stated that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year), and projected that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-shortage\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported in its recent results that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028; the article adds that memory chip prices have surged because supply has not kept pace with the data-center build-out and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half.\", \"links\": [\"clean-8\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-micron-revenue-guidance\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided its next quarter to $50 billion in revenue, which the article states is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\", \"links\": [\"clean-8\"]}]}, {\"n\": 45, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\", \"source_passages\": [\"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; as a share of bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is what is driving up memory and rack costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"According to the article's account of Morgan Stanley Research's analysis, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}, {\"n\": 46, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\", \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Micron reported third-quarter fiscal 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"The article states that Nvidia's latest revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin was 74.9%, and its operating margin was roughly 66%, compared with Micron's 346% growth, 84.6% gross margin, and 80.4% operating margin; it adds that Nvidia still earns more dollars.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 47, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\", \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\", \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"new-kyber-nvl144\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"], \"stored_links\": [\"new-kyber-nvl144\", \"clean-2\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-kyber\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet that houses its Rubin Ultra chips and carries the high end of its data-center value — reach first customer shipments by the end of 2028?\", \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments/deployments of NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or a cloud customer's deployment announcement.\", \"period\": \"2028 check date, based on the item's report (published 2026-07-05) that Kyber has been delayed to 2028.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with the next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at its core remains challenging to manufacture.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\", \"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack architecture was delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\", \"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said a backup plan to bolt two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" which it said could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 48, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-3\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-1\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to debut with its 2027 Vera Rubin Ultra chips (and the NVL576 eight-rack optical system) — reach first shipments in calendar 2027, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber (and the related NVL576 scale-up system) ships in 2027 with Vera Rubin Ultra, versus the reported >12-month delay to 2028.\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — the year Kyber/NVL576 were slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra; the outcome (2027 first shipments vs. a 2028 slip) can be checked through NVIDIA's launch/shipment disclosures during 2027.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028. SemiAnalysis attributed the setback to manufacturing difficulty with the PCB midplane, a specialized multi-layer printed circuit board connecting electronic modules, saying 'Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.'\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576, a larger NVIDIA system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been cancelled after pushback from cloud service providers and hyperscalers over the design and operational burden. The firm said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.'\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report on the Kyber delay and said, 'Our roadmap is intact,' maintaining that its product plan and schedule still stand.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 49, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\", \"source_passages\": [\"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\", \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned for this year (2026), according to the Motley Fool article, which attributes the next-year figure to a claim by Nvidia.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}, {\"n\": 50, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\", \"source_passages\": [\"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\", \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"fool.com reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, and that Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (2027). Both figures are spending projections/claims, not confirmed outlays.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}, {\"n\": 51, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\", \"source_passages\": [\"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\", \"source_passages\": [\"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers (Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta) plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, as reported by fool.com's analyst article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), a step up from the roughly $650 billion they plan to spend in 2026, according to fool.com's article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 52, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\", \"source_passages\": [\"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, according to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent before; memory rises from 5–10% of the GB200 bill of materials to 25–30% on the VR200 while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%, and Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"In the Morgan Stanley Vera Rubin bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, first Nvidia VR200 NVL72 shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is a force now raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and tight memory availability now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation for AI rack builds.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}, {\"n\": 53, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\", \"source_passages\": [\"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\", \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-9\", \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\", \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 54, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"source_passages\": [\"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\", \"clean-5\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\", \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-9\", \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\", \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-10\", \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether proposed U.S. legislation/regulation to phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States is enacted (and its scope and timing).\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The fool.com article's author states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, a figure that could exceed $1 trillion in 2027, and that this will drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology announced that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, as reported by the fool.com article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"The fool.com article states that memory chip prices have skyrocketed on strong data center-driven demand this year, and that new memory capacity at many industry players will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping inventory in low supply for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}]}, {\"n\": 55, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\", \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\", \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"new-kyber-nvl144\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"], \"stored_links\": [\"new-kyber-nvl144\", \"clean-2\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-1\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture (paired with its Vera Rubin Ultra data-center system) begin shipping in 2027 as originally planned, or is it delayed to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Vera Rubin Ultra rack system in 2027 versus a slip to 2028.\", \"period\": \"2027-12-31 check date, based on the item's statement that Kyber had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, with SemiAnalysis reporting a delay to 2028.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a Monday post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane at the system's heart remains challenging to manufacture.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis also said NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan, bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power, has been cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back over its odd design and heavy operational burden.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber architecture had been delayed, saying, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 56, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"E1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\", \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"NH1\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"E3\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"], \"stored_links\": [\"NH1\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-kyber\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra data-center chips, begin shipping in calendar 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether first Kyber (Rubin Ultra) rack shipments occur in calendar 2027 or are delayed to 2028.\", \"period\": \"Whether Kyber ships in calendar 2027 (original plan) or slips to 2028 — check at end of calendar 2027, the year the item says it had been slated to debut.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-kyber-delay\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-backup-plan-cancelled\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together for similar compute was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving Nvidia with what the firm called \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\"; it predicted this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-nvidia-denial\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report of a Kyber delay, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-rubin-shipping\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-dc-revenue-estimate\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 57, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-3\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-1\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144), the cabinet that debuts with Vera Rubin Ultra and carries the 2027 high-end platform's value, reach first shipments — the step that brings revenue — by the end of calendar 2027, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra systems reach first shipments by the end of calendar 2027.\", \"period\": \"End of calendar 2027: the 2027 debut the item says Kyber had been slated for, and which SemiAnalysis now reports has slipped more than 12 months to 2028.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips with 144 GPUs per unit, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, attributing the slip to manufacturability problems with the multi-layer PCB midplane at the system's core; it added that the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together to reach comparable scale-up power for Rubin Ultra has been cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, which the firm said leaves Nvidia with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra and could open a rare high-end technical opening for AMD and Google's in-house chips.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report on Kyber delays and said its roadmap is intact.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 58, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\", \"source_passages\": [\"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-6\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates, in a bill-of-materials analysis, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the generation-over-generation increase.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content in Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory rose from 5 to 10 percent of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis has first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale writes that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it says memory availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}, {\"n\": 59, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\", \"source_passages\": [\"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-4\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\", \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-memory-price\", \"question\": \"What will average DRAM (memory chip) contract prices be in calendar 2027, given industry supply that the item says stays tight for at least the next year and a half and possibly until 2028?\", \"outcome\": \"The average DRAM/memory contract price level in calendar 2027 versus 2026, a key input cost for NVIDIA's GPUs (HBM/DRAM), reflecting whether tight memory supply keeps lifting NVIDIA's memory input costs.\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2027 (the item's 'next year'); the shortage is described as lasting 'at least the next year and a half' with tight conditions possibly until 2028.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-hyperscaler-capex\", \"summary\": \"The article (Keithen Drury, fool.com) states that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026), and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which the author says will drive even higher demand for NVIDIA Gpus.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-shortage\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that memory demand from the data-center build-out has outstripped supply and driven memory chip prices up sharply; it states that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving supply tight for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher, and relays Micron's statement that tight market conditions could last until 2028. DRAM is described as high-speed memory used by NVIDIA's GPUs, so this is an input-cost signal for NVIDIA.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-advance-orders\", \"summary\": \"The article states that many of NVIDIA's clients are placing orders for devices well in advance of when they need them, so they can bring capacity online quickly once data-center infrastructure is ready, which the author says gives NVIDIA a strong read on data-center build-out plans.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 60, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"stored_reference_items\": [{\"stored_id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"stored_id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"], \"stored_links\": [\"clean-3\"]}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-kyber-2028\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack that houses Rubin Ultra GPUs and carries the next generation of its high-end AI systems — reach first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in calendar 2028, as SemiAnalysis now reports, rather than in 2027?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber NVL144 reaches first shipments with Vera Rubin Ultra in calendar 2028, i.e. whether the more-than-12-month delay to 2028 reported by SemiAnalysis holds or Nvidia's 'roadmap is intact' denial is borne out.\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2028 — the year the item reports Kyber is now delayed to, versus a planned 2027 debut with Vera Rubin Ultra; set by the item's reported delay date.\"}], \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a Monday post that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture — the 144-GPU cabinet designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028; it had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. The cause, per SemiAnalysis, is manufacturability of the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-2028\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-2028\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar scale-up power — was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving Nvidia with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.' The firm said that could give AMD and Google, whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-2028\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"According to the report, Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a larger Nvidia system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-2028\"]}]}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-2.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",e[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"usable\\\",e[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"claims\\\",[(c[\\\"claim_id\\\"],c[\\\"claim\\\"]) for c in e[\\\"claims\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\",e[\\\"link_rule\\\"]),print(\\\"theses\\\",[(t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"]) for t in e[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+e[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in t[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"capex\\\",\\\"kyber\\\",\\\"memory\\\",\\\"huawei\\\",\\\"credit\\\"])]),print(\\\"evidence\\\",[(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"summary\\\"],x[\\\"links\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]])) for e in p[3:16]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-2.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",e[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"usable\\\",e[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"claims\\\",[(c[\\\"claim_id\\\"],c[\\\"claim\\\"]) for c in e[\\\"claims\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\",e[\\\"link_rule\\\"]),print(\\\"theses\\\",[(t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"]) for t in e[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+e[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in t[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"capex\\\",\\\"kyber\\\",\\\"memory\\\",\\\"huawei\\\",\\\"credit\\\"])]),print(\\\"evidence\\\",[(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"summary\\\"],x[\\\"links\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]])) for e in p[3:16]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n 34 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?'), ('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nevidence [('ev1', 'In a 7 July 2026 fool.com article, author Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year and that next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he said would drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.', ['clean-6']), ('ev2', 'The 7 July 2026 fool.com article said Nvidia has a strong read on data center build-out plans because many clients are placing orders for its devices well in advance of when they need them, so they can bring them online quickly once data center infrastructure is ready.', ['clean-6']), ('ev3', 'The 7 July 2026 fool.com article said supply of NAND and DRAM memory has not kept up with data center-driven demand, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed (benefiting Micron), and that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later — leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.', [])]\n\n 35 usable True claims [('G', 'Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).')] rule The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', 'How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?'), ('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"), ('clean-6', \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"), ('new-mem-prices-2026', 'How much will memory prices — the high-bandwidth memory (HBM) and DRAM that AI accelerators and consumer devices depend on — rise over calendar 2026?')]\nevidence [('e1', 'NVIDIA\\'s latest reported figures, cited by Allen Harris in a comparison with Micron, showed revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%; the item does not say which quarter these NVIDIA figures cover, and notes that NVIDIA \"still earns more dollars\" than Micron.', []), ('e2', 'Goldman Sachs, cited by Allen Harris, estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.', ['clean-4']), ('e3', 'Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% revenue growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a GAAP gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and an operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%), as cited by Allen Harris; the item frames Micron as a memory maker supplying the memory that sits alongside AI accelerators.', ['new-mem-prices-2026']), ('e4', 'Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, according to Allen Harris.', ['new-mem-prices-2026'])]\n\n 36 usable True claims [('M1', 'Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.'), ('M2', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.'), ('M3', \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\")] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', 'How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?'), ('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nevidence [('ev1', \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. It puts memory content at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. These are Morgan Stanley estimates, not NVIDIA disclosures.\", []), ('ev2', \"Morgan Stanley's analysis, reported by MarketScale, says first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter. This is a third-party schedule estimate, not an NVIDIA-confirmed shipment.\", ['clean-2']), ('ev3', \"Morgan Stanley's analysis, reported by MarketScale, attributes the Vera Rubin rack's cost to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, notes contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and estimates that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack. These are Morgan Stanley estimates and market commentary.\", [])]\n\n 37 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?'), ('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"), ('new-memory-prices-2027', 'Will DRAM and HBM memory contract prices be higher in calendar 2027 than in 2026, given that new memory capacity is not expected to come online until 2027 or later and Micron expects tight market conditions to last until 2028?')]\nevidence [('ev-capex-650b-1t', \"fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\", ['clean-6']), ('ev-micron-tight-2028', \"Micron Technology, in its most recent quarterly announcement as reported by fool.com, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside what the article calls huge growth and strong guidance for the following quarter (attribution is to Micron's announcement, relayed by the article).\", ['new-memory-prices-2027']), ('ev-memory-capacity-2027', \"The article's author (fool.com, Keithen Drury) forecasts that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half, and that this could push memory chip prices even higher because demand is not slowing.\", ['new-memory-prices-2027', 'clean-8'])]\n\n 38 usable True claims [('M1', 'Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.'), ('M2', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.'), ('M3', \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\")] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', 'How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?'), ('clean-2', \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?')]\nevidence [('e1', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", ['clean-4']), ('e2', \"In its bill-of-materials analysis, Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", []), ('e3', \"The item reports that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", ['clean-3']), ('e4', 'The item reports that constrained DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory supply against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.', [])]\n\n 39 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', 'How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?'), ('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"), ('clean-6', \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\")]\nevidence [('ev-1', 'The article reports that in calendar 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures.', ['clean-4']), ('ev-2', 'NVIDIA claims that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), according to the article.', ['clean-4'])]\n\n 40 usable True claims [('G', 'Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).')] rule The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?'), ('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nevidence [('ev-goldman-capex', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by the article.', ['clean-6']), ('ev-micron-q3fy26', 'Memory maker Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%).', ['clean-6'])]\n\n 41 usable True claims [('M1', 'Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.'), ('M2', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.'), ('M3', \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\")] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', 'How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?'), ('clean-2', \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?')]\nevidence [('e1', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimated that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimated Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", []), ('e2', \"Morgan Stanley put memory content at about $2 million per Vera Rubin rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the rack bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", []), ('e3', \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to Morgan Stanley's analysis reported by MarketScale.\", ['clean-3']), ('e4', 'MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, and that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.', [])]\n\n 42 usable True claims [('M1', 'Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.'), ('M2', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.'), ('M3', \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\")] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', 'How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?'), ('clean-2', \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?')]\nevidence [('ev1', \"Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. These are analyst bill-of-materials estimates, not disclosed NVIDIA prices or a reported transaction.\", ['clean-4']), ('ev2', \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, reported by MarketScale, puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", []), ('ev3', \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) rack first shipments are reported as scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per MarketScale's article drawing on Morgan Stanley's analysis. This is a shipment schedule/plan, not a confirmed shipment.\", ['clean-3'])]\n\n 43 usable True claims [('G', 'Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).')] rule The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', 'How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?'), ('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"), ('clean-6', \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\")]\nevidence [('e1', \"Columnist Allen Harris (Berkshire Edge) reports NVIDIA's own latest-quarter financial metrics alongside Micron's: NVIDIA revenue growth of 85% (versus Micron's ~346%), GAAP gross margin of 74.9% (versus Micron's 84.6%) and operating margin of roughly 66% (versus Micron's 80.4%), adding that NVIDIA still earns more dollars. The period of NVIDIA's figures is not specified beyond 'latest'.\", []), ('e2', \"Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge, who attributes the AI-driven memory demand to NVIDIA's success spilling over into Micron.\", ['clean-4']), ('e3', 'Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.', ['clean-4']), ('e4', 'Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.', [])]\n\n 44 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?'), ('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nevidence [('ev-capex', 'In a fool.com column, analyst Keithen Drury stated that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year), and projected that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion.', ['clean-6']), ('ev-memory-shortage', 'Micron Technology reported in its recent results that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028; the article adds that memory chip prices have surged because supply has not kept pace with the data-center build-out and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half.', ['clean-8']), ('ev-micron-revenue-guidance', 'Micron Technology guided its next quarter to $50 billion in revenue, which the article states is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.', ['clean-8'])]\n\n 45 usable True claims [('M1', 'Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.')] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?'), ('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nevidence [('ev-1', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", ['clean-6']), ('ev-2', 'Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.', []), ('ev-3', \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; as a share of bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\", []), ('ev-4', 'The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is what is driving up memory and rack costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.', ['clean-3']), ('ev-5', \"According to the article's account of Morgan Stanley Research's analysis, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", ['clean-3'])]\n\n 46 usable True claims [('G', 'Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).')] rule The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-1', 'How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?'), ('clean-2', \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"), ('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('clean-5', \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"), ('clean-6', \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nevidence [('e1', 'Micron reported third-quarter fiscal 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.', ['clean-5']), ('e2', \"The article states that Nvidia's latest revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin was 74.9%, and its operating margin was roughly 66%, compared with Micron's 346% growth, 84.6% gross margin, and 80.4% operating margin; it adds that Nvidia still earns more dollars.\", []), ('e3', 'Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.', ['clean-5']), ('e4', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.', ['clean-4'])]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-2.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",e[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"usable\\\",e[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"claims\\\",[(c[\\\"claim_id\\\"],c[\\\"claim\\\"]) for c in e[\\\"claims\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\",e[\\\"link_rule\\\"]),print(\\\"theses\\\",[(t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"]) for t in e[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+e[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in t[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"capex\\\",\\\"kyber\\\",\\\"memory\\\"])]),print(\\\"evidence\\\",[(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"summary\\\"],x[\\\"links\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]])) for e in p[16:27]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-2.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",e[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"usable\\\",e[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"claims\\\",[(c[\\\"claim_id\\\"],c[\\\"claim\\\"]) for c in e[\\\"claims\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\",e[\\\"link_rule\\\"]),print(\\\"theses\\\",[(t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"]) for t in e[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+e[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in t[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"capex\\\",\\\"kyber\\\",\\\"memory\\\"])]),print(\\\"evidence\\\",[(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"summary\\\"],x[\\\"links\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]])) for e in p[16:27]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n 47 usable True claims [('K1', \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\"), ('K2', 'NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\ntheses [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('new-kyber', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet that houses its Rubin Ultra chips and carries the high end of its data-center value — reach first customer shipments by the end of 2028?\")]\nevidence [('e1', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with the next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at its core remains challenging to manufacture.\", ['new-kyber', 'clean-2']), ('e2', 'Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis\\'s report that its Kyber rack architecture was delayed to 2028, stating \"Our roadmap is intact.\"', ['new-kyber', 'clean-2']), ('e3', \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", ['new-kyber']), ('e4', 'SemiAnalysis said a backup plan to bolt two of Nvidia\\'s current-generation racks together for similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" which it said could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end.', ['new-kyber']), ('e5', \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", ['clean-2']), ('e6', \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", [])]\n\n 48 usable True claims [('K1', \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\"), ('K2', 'NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\ntheses [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('new-1', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to debut with its 2027 Vera Rubin Ultra chips (and the NVL576 eight-rack optical system) — reach first shipments in calendar 2027, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nevidence [('e1', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028. SemiAnalysis attributed the setback to manufacturing difficulty with the PCB midplane, a specialized multi-layer printed circuit board connecting electronic modules, saying 'Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.'\", ['new-1']), ('e2', 'SemiAnalysis said NVL576, a larger NVIDIA system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.', ['new-1']), ('e3', \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been cancelled after pushback from cloud service providers and hyperscalers over the design and operational burden. The firm said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.'\", ['new-1']), ('e4', \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report on the Kyber delay and said, 'Our roadmap is intact,' maintaining that its product plan and schedule still stand.\", ['new-1']), ('e5', \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", ['clean-3']), ('e6', \"SemiAnalysis projected that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", [])]\n\n 49 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nevidence [('e1', 'Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned for this year (2026), according to the Motley Fool article, which attributes the next-year figure to a claim by Nvidia.', ['clean-6'])]\n\n 50 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nevidence [('e1', 'fool.com reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, and that Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (2027). Both figures are spending projections/claims, not confirmed outlays.', ['clean-6'])]\n\n 51 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nevidence [('e1', \"The big four AI hyperscalers (Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta) plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, as reported by fool.com's analyst article.\", ['clean-4']), ('e2', \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), a step up from the roughly $650 billion they plan to spend in 2026, according to fool.com's article.\", ['clean-4'])]\n\n 52 usable True claims [('M1', 'Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.')] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\ntheses [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nevidence [('ev-1', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, according to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale.\", ['clean-6']), ('ev-2', \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent before; memory rises from 5–10% of the GB200 bill of materials to 25–30% on the VR200 while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%, and Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", ['clean-6']), ('ev-3', 'In the Morgan Stanley Vera Rubin bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, first Nvidia VR200 NVL72 shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.', ['clean-3']), ('ev-4', 'MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is a force now raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and tight memory availability now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation for AI rack builds.', ['clean-3'])]\n\n 53 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('clean-5', \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"), ('clean-6', \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nevidence [('e1', 'The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).', ['clean-4'])]\n\n 54 usable True claims [('C26', 'About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.'), ('C27', 'Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\ntheses [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('clean-5', \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"), ('clean-6', \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nevidence [('e1', \"The fool.com article's author states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, a figure that could exceed $1 trillion in 2027, and that this will drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", ['clean-4']), ('e2', 'Micron Technology announced that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, as reported by the fool.com article.', ['clean-5']), ('e3', 'The fool.com article states that memory chip prices have skyrocketed on strong data center-driven demand this year, and that new memory capacity at many industry players will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping inventory in low supply for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.', ['clean-5'])]\n\n 55 usable True claims [('K1', \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\"), ('K2', 'NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\ntheses [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('new-1', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture (paired with its Vera Rubin Ultra data-center system) begin shipping in 2027 as originally planned, or is it delayed to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nevidence [('e1', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a Monday post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane at the system's heart remains challenging to manufacture.\", ['new-1']), ('e2', 'SemiAnalysis also said NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.', ['new-1']), ('e3', \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan, bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power, has been cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back over its odd design and heavy operational burden.\", ['new-1']), ('e4', 'NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis\\'s report that its Kyber architecture had been delayed, saying, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"', ['new-1']), ('e5', \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", ['clean-2']), ('e6', \"SemiAnalysis projects NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", [])]\n\n 56 usable True claims [('K1', \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\"), ('K2', 'NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\ntheses [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('new-kyber', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra data-center chips, begin shipping in calendar 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028?\")]\nevidence [('ev-kyber-delay', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", ['new-kyber']), ('ev-backup-plan-cancelled', 'SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia\\'s backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together for similar compute was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving Nvidia with what the firm called \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\"; it predicted this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.', ['new-kyber']), ('ev-nvidia-denial', 'Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report of a Kyber delay, stating \"Our roadmap is intact.\"', ['new-kyber']), ('ev-rubin-shipping', \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", ['clean-3']), ('ev-dc-revenue-estimate', \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", [])]\n\n 57 usable True claims [('K1', \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\"), ('K2', 'NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.')] rule Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\ntheses [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('new-1', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144), the cabinet that debuts with Vera Rubin Ultra and carries the 2027 high-end platform's value, reach first shipments — the step that brings revenue — by the end of calendar 2027, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nevidence [('e1', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips with 144 GPUs per unit, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, attributing the slip to manufacturability problems with the multi-layer PCB midplane at the system's core; it added that the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", ['new-1']), ('e2', \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together to reach comparable scale-up power for Rubin Ultra has been cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, which the firm said leaves Nvidia with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra and could open a rare high-end technical opening for AMD and Google's in-house chips.\", ['new-1']), ('e3', 'Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report on Kyber delays and said its roadmap is intact.', ['new-1']), ('e4', \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", ['clean-3']), ('e5', \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", [])]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
